1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,456
In this lesson, we'll count outcomes
without listing them all.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:09,998
If one choice has m options and a second
has n, together there are m times n

3
00:00:09,998 --> 00:00:10,637
outcomes.

4
00:00:11,087 --> 00:00:15,397
Three shirts and four pants make three
times four, or twelve outfits.

5
00:00:16,533 --> 00:00:22,485
Five factorial means five times four times
three times two times one, which is one

6
00:00:22,485 --> 00:00:23,573
hundred twenty.

7
00:00:24,023 --> 00:00:29,084
It counts the ways to line up five
different books. By definition, zero

8
00:00:29,084 --> 00:00:30,295
factorial is one.

9
00:00:31,433 --> 00:00:34,740
A permutation is an arrangement where
order matters.

10
00:00:35,190 --> 00:00:41,434
The number of ways to arrange r items
chosen from n is n P r, equal to n

11
00:00:41,434 --> 00:00:44,470
factorial over n minus r factorial.

12
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:49,269
How many ways can eight runners finish
first, second and third?

13
00:00:49,719 --> 00:00:54,999
Eight P three equals eight times seven
times six, which is three hundred thirty

14
00:00:54,999 --> 00:00:55,266
six.

15
00:00:56,400 --> 00:01:01,613
When letters repeat, divide out the
duplicates. The letters of LEVEL: five

16
00:01:01,613 --> 00:01:03,867
letters, with two Ls and two Es.

17
00:01:04,317 --> 00:01:09,999
Five factorial over two factorial times
two factorial is one twenty over four,

18
00:01:09,999 --> 00:01:11,165
which is thirty.

19
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:17,783
Your turn. How many four digit codes use
digits zero through nine with no repeats?

20
00:01:18,233 --> 00:01:19,769
Pause the video and try it.

21
00:01:20,219 --> 00:01:26,232
Ten P four equals ten times nine times
eight times seven, which is five thousand

22
00:01:26,232 --> 00:01:26,683
forty.

23
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:31,512
In this lesson, we'll count selections
where order doesn't matter.

24
00:01:32,633 --> 00:01:36,409
Picking Ana, then Ben, is the same team as
Ben, then Ana.

25
00:01:36,859 --> 00:01:39,761
So a combination counts groups, not
orderings.

26
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:47,702
The number of ways to choose r items from
n is n C r equals n factorial over r

27
00:01:47,702 --> 00:01:50,841
factorial times n minus r factorial.

28
00:01:51,291 --> 00:01:56,795
It's the permutation count divided by r
factorial, the ways to order each group.

29
00:01:57,933 --> 00:02:01,603
How many ways can you choose a committee
of three from ten people?

30
00:02:02,053 --> 00:02:07,385
Ten C three equals ten times nine times
eight over six, which is one hundred

31
00:02:07,385 --> 00:02:07,877
twenty.

32
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,136
Choose two boys from six and two girls
from five.

33
00:02:12,586 --> 00:02:18,542
Six C two is fifteen and five C two is
ten, so fifteen times ten gives one

34
00:02:18,542 --> 00:02:19,669
hundred fifty.

35
00:02:19,800 --> 00:02:24,299
Ask yourself: does swapping two people
change the outcome?

36
00:02:24,749 --> 00:02:29,876
Electing a president and vice president,
order matters, so it's a permutation.

37
00:02:29,876 --> 00:02:33,623
Picking two delegates, it doesn't, so it's
a combination.

38
00:02:34,767 --> 00:02:39,076
Your turn. How many five card hands come
from a fifty two card deck?

39
00:02:39,526 --> 00:02:41,062
Pause the video and try it.

40
00:02:41,512 --> 00:02:46,945
Fifty two C five equals two million, five
hundred ninety eight thousand, nine

41
00:02:46,945 --> 00:02:47,933
hundred sixty.

42
00:02:49,066 --> 00:02:53,973
In this lesson, we'll find probabilities
when more than one event is involved.

43
00:02:55,099 --> 00:03:00,682
The probability of an event is favorable
outcomes over total outcomes, when all are

44
00:03:00,682 --> 00:03:01,691
equally likely.

45
00:03:02,141 --> 00:03:06,067
Rolling an even number on a die: three out
of six, or one half.

46
00:03:07,199 --> 00:03:13,109
The complement of A is everything that is
not A. P of not A equals one minus P of A.

47
00:03:13,559 --> 00:03:18,401
The chance of not rolling a six is one
minus one sixth, or five sixths.

48
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:24,375
P of A or B equals P of A plus P of B
minus P of A and B.

49
00:03:24,825 --> 00:03:30,626
Drawing a heart or a king: thirteen over
fifty two plus four over fifty two minus

50
00:03:30,626 --> 00:03:35,855
one over fifty two, which is sixteen over
fifty two, or four thirteenths.

51
00:03:36,999 --> 00:03:41,671
For independent events, P of A and B
equals P of A times P of B.

52
00:03:42,121 --> 00:03:47,305
Flipping heads and rolling a six: one half
times one sixth, which is one twelfth.

53
00:03:48,433 --> 00:03:52,699
Counting helps too. Choose two cards:
what's the chance both are aces?

54
00:03:53,149 --> 00:03:59,402
Four C two over fifty two C two equals six
over one thousand three hundred twenty

55
00:03:59,402 --> 00:04:02,259
six, about point zero zero four five.

56
00:04:03,399 --> 00:04:07,367
Your turn. Flip three coins. What's the
chance of at least one head?

57
00:04:07,817 --> 00:04:09,353
Pause the video and try it.

58
00:04:09,803 --> 00:04:13,665
Use the complement: one minus one eighth,
which is seven eighths.

59
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:19,621
In this lesson, we'll find probabilities
when we already know something happened.

60
00:04:20,767 --> 00:04:24,073
P of A given B is the chance of A, knowing
B occurred.

61
00:04:24,523 --> 00:04:28,150
Knowing B shrinks the sample space to just
the B outcomes.

62
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:34,441
The formula is P of A given B equals P of
A and B over P of B.

63
00:04:34,891 --> 00:04:40,432
Roll a die. Given the roll is even, what's
the chance it's a six? One sixth over one

64
00:04:40,432 --> 00:04:42,081
half, which is one third.

65
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:48,959
In a survey of one hundred students, sixty
play a sport, and forty of those also play

66
00:04:48,959 --> 00:04:49,365
music.

67
00:04:49,815 --> 00:04:55,131
Given a student plays a sport, the
probability they play music is forty over

68
00:04:55,131 --> 00:04:56,599
sixty, or two thirds.

69
00:04:57,733 --> 00:05:00,720
Drawing without replacement makes events
dependent.

70
00:05:01,170 --> 00:05:06,436
Two cards, both hearts: thirteen over
fifty two, times twelve over fifty one,

71
00:05:06,436 --> 00:05:08,146
which is one seventeenth.

72
00:05:09,267 --> 00:05:13,512
A and B are independent when P of A given
B equals P of A.

73
00:05:13,962 --> 00:05:19,259
Suppose thirty of the forty non-athletes
also play music. Then seventy of one

74
00:05:19,259 --> 00:05:24,762
hundred students play music overall, but
only two thirds of athletes do. Seventy

75
00:05:24,762 --> 00:05:28,063
percent is not two thirds, so they're
dependent.

76
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:33,960
Your turn. A bag has five red and three
blue marbles. Draw two without

77
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:36,816
replacement. Find the chance both are red.

78
00:05:37,266 --> 00:05:38,802
Pause the video and try it.

79
00:05:39,252 --> 00:05:43,961
Five eighths times four sevenths, which is
twenty over fifty six, or five

80
00:05:43,961 --> 00:05:44,735
fourteenths.

81
00:05:45,866 --> 00:05:49,855
In this lesson, we'll study the bell
shaped normal distribution.

82
00:05:50,999 --> 00:05:56,195
Many measurements, like heights or test
scores, pile up around an average and thin

83
00:05:56,195 --> 00:05:57,335
out on both sides.

84
00:05:57,785 --> 00:06:00,452
That symmetric bell shape is the normal
curve.

85
00:06:01,599 --> 00:06:06,463
The mean, mu, is the center. The standard
deviation, sigma, measures spread.

86
00:06:06,913 --> 00:06:09,857
A larger sigma makes the bell wider and
flatter.

87
00:06:10,999 --> 00:06:16,185
The empirical rule: about sixty eight
percent of data falls within one standard

88
00:06:16,185 --> 00:06:21,568
deviation of the mean, ninety five percent
within two, and ninety nine point seven

89
00:06:21,568 --> 00:06:22,946
percent within three.

90
00:06:23,396 --> 00:06:27,364
That's the sixty eight, ninety five,
ninety nine point seven rule.

91
00:06:28,499 --> 00:06:34,327
A z score tells how many standard
deviations a value is from the mean: z

92
00:06:34,327 --> 00:06:36,755
equals x minus mu, over sigma.

93
00:06:37,205 --> 00:06:43,140
Scores have mean seventy and sigma eight.
A score of eighty six has z equals sixteen

94
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:44,907
over eight, which is two.

95
00:06:46,033 --> 00:06:48,038
What percent score above eighty six?

96
00:06:48,488 --> 00:06:54,457
Ninety five percent are within two sigma,
so five percent are outside, split evenly.

97
00:06:54,457 --> 00:06:58,152
That leaves two point five percent above
eighty six.

98
00:06:59,299 --> 00:07:04,704
Your turn. Heights have mean sixty six
inches and sigma three. What percent are

99
00:07:04,704 --> 00:07:07,577
between sixty three and sixty nine inches?

100
00:07:08,027 --> 00:07:09,563
Pause the video and try it.

101
00:07:10,013 --> 00:07:14,109
That's within one standard deviation, so
about sixty eight percent.

102
00:07:15,233 --> 00:07:19,695
In this lesson, we'll see how a small
sample can tell us about a whole

103
00:07:19,695 --> 00:07:20,396
population.

104
00:07:21,533 --> 00:07:26,994
The population is everyone you care about.
A sample is the part you actually measure.

105
00:07:27,444 --> 00:07:31,839
A statistic from the sample estimates a
parameter of the population.

106
00:07:32,966 --> 00:07:37,894
A simple random sample gives every member
an equal chance of being chosen.

107
00:07:38,344 --> 00:07:43,699
Surveying only your friends, or only
people who volunteer, introduces bias.

108
00:07:44,833 --> 00:07:50,625
A survey asks questions. An observational
study watches without interfering. An

109
00:07:50,625 --> 00:07:53,558
experiment assigns treatments at random.

110
00:07:54,008 --> 00:07:57,550
Only a randomized experiment can show
cause and effect.

111
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:04,050
For a sample proportion, the margin of
error is about one over the square root of

112
00:08:04,050 --> 00:08:04,182
n.

113
00:08:04,632 --> 00:08:08,643
With four hundred people, that's one over
twenty, or five percent.

114
00:08:09,766 --> 00:08:14,417
A poll of four hundred voters finds fifty
four percent support a measure.

115
00:08:14,867 --> 00:08:20,846
The interval is fifty four plus or minus
five, so forty nine to fifty nine percent.

116
00:08:20,846 --> 00:08:25,384
Since it includes values below fifty, the
result isn't certain.

117
00:08:26,533 --> 00:08:31,540
Your turn. Estimate the margin of error
for a sample of two thousand five hundred

118
00:08:31,540 --> 00:08:31,973
people.

119
00:08:32,423 --> 00:08:33,959
Pause the video and try it.

120
00:08:34,409 --> 00:08:39,678
One over the square root of twenty five
hundred is one over fifty, or two percent.

121
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:45,715
